  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAnother mild November afternoon with temperatures in the middle\n50s to middle 60s. The coolest readings were near Marshfield where\ncooler air was starting to advect into our southwest counties.\nRadar continued to show scattered showers across northeast WI \nearly this afternoon. A few showers were approaching 50 dBZ, \nthus would not be surprised if there was a few rumbles of thunder. \n\nFor the rest of the afternoon into tonight. convection should\nremain scattered in nature as cold front and upper closed low\nover Minnesota opens up and moves into the area overnight into\nThursday. A steadier round of rain is expected across the north \nlate tonight into Thursday morning in association with the upper \ntrough, with more scattered showers to the south. There is a\n50-90% chance of an additional 0.25\" of rain tonight into Thursday\nacross northern Wisconsin where the worse drought conditions \nexists.\n\nAs the cold front moves across the area on Thursday, gusty northwest\nwinds will develop from west to east during the day Thursday and\ncontinue into Thursday night. Wind gusts of 25 to 40 mph are expected,\nwith locally higher gusts over northern Door County. There is a \n40-60% chance of wind gusts to 45 mph across northern Door County \nThursday afternoon and evening. If winds reach this speed, a Wind\nAdvisory may be needed for Door County. Meanwhile, the northwest\nwinds may bring a chance of snow showers to far northern Wisconsin\nThursday night. Latest forecast has a dusting of snow across the\nnorth, especially Vilas County. So far this fall, only a few\nflurries were noted across the far north in October. \n\nLatest model data would suggest tranquil conditions this weekend\nthrough the middle of next week. Near normal temperatures are\nexpected on Friday and Saturday, then above normal temperatures \nnext week. The latest 8-14 Day temperature outlook (Nov 11-17) \nfrom the Climate Prediction Center indicated a 97% chance of near \nor above normal temperatures at Green Bay, Rhinelander and Wausau.\nThat means there is only a 3% chance of below normal temperatures.",
